# The extractor answers two questions about a module and nothing else: WHAT DOES IT EXPOSE,
# and WHICH COMMENT DOCUMENTS EACH OF THOSE DECLARATIONS. It renders nothing. There is no
# indent here, no column budget, no colour, no terminal — `zerg doc`'s typography is
# cmd/doc_render.zg's, and issue #20's HTML will be a SECOND rendering of exactly what comes
# out of here. A fact about the source that only one of those two could read would be a fact
# the other one has to re-derive, which is how a project ends up with two extractors that
# disagree about what a module contains.
#
# It lives in this module rather than beside the command because of one function:
# `tokenize_trivia` (lexer.zg) is private here, and it is the only honest way to find a
# comment. The alternative — scanning the source text for `#` — reads the `#` inside a string
# literal as the start of a comment, and a documentation tool that lies about the source is
# worse than no documentation tool. The lexer already separates the two, already separates a
# `#[…]` decorator (Kind.Hash) from a comment, and already carries a line number on every
# token, so the attachment below is line arithmetic over a stream that has done the reading.
#
# WHAT A COMMENT IS ATTACHED TO is decided by geometry, in two passes over that stream:
#
#   1. the token stream is reduced to three facts per line — is there a doc RUN ending here,
#      does this line carry CODE, is this line inside a DECORATOR — and a run is a block of
#      whole-line comments on consecutive lines. A comment with code before it on its line is
#      a trailing note (`}  # so far so good`) and is dropped: it belongs to the line it sits
#      on, and stitching it onto whatever is declared below would attribute a remark about a
#      closing brace to the next function.
#
#   2. every declaration — private ones included — claims the run that ENDS ON THE LINE ABOVE
#      IT, stepping over any `#[derive(Eq)]` in between, since a decorator standing between a
#      comment and its declaration does not break the adjacency a reader sees. A blank line
#      does break it, and needs no rule of its own: a blank line emits no token, so the run
#      simply ends further up than the line above.
#
# EVERY COMMENT IS READ TWICE, ONCE FOR EACH OF ITS READERS (#18). A `##` line is the READER'S
# text — what someone calling the declaration needs — and a `#` line is the MAINTAINER'S, which
# is where a comment in this tree says why the code is the way it is. The marker is per LINE, so
# one run can hold both, and it is what GRAMMAR's DOC-COMMENT has always said: `##` documents,
# and any other `#` is an ordinary comment. So a run yields its reader text (`doc_reader`) and
# the whole run as written, and a declaration whose run holds no `##` at all is UNDOCUMENTED
# however much was written above it — one rule, which `--check` holds, so a forgotten `##` is a
# finding rather than a page that quietly went blank.
#
# A RUN THAT OPENS WITH A SECTION BANNER — `# --- levels ------` — DOCUMENTS NOTHING, and the
# WHOLE run goes, not just the banner line. A banner is a mark on the FILE: it divides the
# source into chapters for someone reading the source, and what a chapter is called is not what
# the first declaration under it is called. What follows the banner is the CHAPTER'S prose, and
# it is prose about the group — `log.zg`'s "the two formats, and where each is chosen" goes on
# to talk about `Format`, about `Logger.col`, and about which default zerolog chose — so
# keeping it and dropping only the dashes would hand one declaration a paragraph written about
# several. Dropped, `enum Level` reads as undocumented, which is what it is; kept, it read as
# documented by 89 characters of dashes, which is the failure this tool exists to prevent.
#
# The private declarations take part in pass 2 although nothing prints them, because the
# MODULE HEADER is defined as the run nothing claimed: the first run in the file, with no code
# above it, still unclaimed once every declaration has taken its own. That is what tells
# `strings.zg`'s file header — which sits flush against `import "ascii"`, with no blank line
# anywhere near it — from a comment that documents the first declaration. An import claims
# nothing: it binds a namespace, it declares no member of this module's surface, and a reader
# looking at the top of a file is reading about the file.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY IS NOT:
#
#   - a second type printer. Every type is spelled by `ty_name` (ast.zg) and every
#     spec-with-arguments by `spec_key`, because a signature in a document that disagreed with
#     a signature in a diagnostic would be one of them lying about the language.
#   - a second parser. `doc_lit` prints a default value only when it is a LITERAL, reading the
#     surface text the literal node already carries, and prints `…` for everything else. A
#     document's signature answers "how do I call this"; it does not promise to evaluate.
#   - an evaluator of examples. A ```zerg``` fence is carried through as written. Running one
#     is `--check`'s job.
#   - silent about what it could not read. A file whose parse RAISES still yields its header —
#     the header rule needs no AST — and the failure is recorded as `DocFile.ok` for the
#     renderer to speak about. `src/stdlib/atomic.zg` is the live case: E9038 comes from the
#     PARSER (a generic struct), so a doc tool that let the raise through would drop a whole
#     module, and one that swallowed it would drop the module's declarations without saying so.
#   - the author of that sentence. What could not be read is a FACT and its wording is not: the
#     path a file was reached by is one this compiler resolved (a stdlib file's is absolute and
#     differs per installation), so how to name it to a reader, and where to send them, belong
#     to whoever is writing for that reader. It is `hidden`'s argument, one struct up.

import (
	"ascii"

	"./ast"
	"./emit"
	"./lexer"
	"./parser"
	"./token"
)

# --- what a document is -------------------------------------------------------------------

# DocModule is one module's whole document. It is extracted once and rendered many ways, so it
# carries what the SOURCE says and nothing about how it is shown.
pub struct DocModule {
	# the name the reader asked for, which is the document's title
	pub name: str

	# the module's source files, in the order they were handed over — which is the order
	# `__zrt_listdir` answers a directory in, and that is sorted. A module is ONE file far
	# more often than it is several, and the several are what this list exists for: a
	# directory module is a document with several chapters, and a chapter is a file.
	pub files: list[DocFile]

	# every exposed declaration, in SOURCE ORDER within each file and file order across them
	# — the order the author put them in, which this tree treats as part of the document
	# (`strings.zg` keeps the trim family together, and its header narrates the file in the
	# order the file is written). Finding one declaration by name is `doc_find`'s job, and
	# alphabetical order is that question's, not this list's.
	#
	# It is ONE FLAT LIST and not a list per file, because every question asked of it is
	# asked across the module: a method is grouped under its type wherever that type was
	# written, and `doc_find` reaches a name without being told which chapter holds it.
	# Which file a declaration came from is `DocDecl.path`, which a renderer groups by.
	pub decls: list[DocDecl]

	# every worked example written in any comment of the module, in source order — attached
	# or not. An example is a claim about the code wherever it is written, and one that
	# `--check` skipped for sitting in a header nobody claims would be a claim nobody checks.
	pub examples: list[DocExample]

	# every comment line SHAPED like an example fence that is not one: an indented fence, a
	# fence with words after `zerg`, a `zg` fence, an `output` fence with no example above it.
	# Skipping them would be the same silence one step earlier: a reader sees an example, and
	# nothing ran it.
	pub strays: list[DocStray]
}

# DocStray is one line that looks like a fence and is not one, where it was written.
pub struct DocStray {
	pub path: str
	pub line: int
	pub text: str

	# a correctly spelled ` ```output ` fence standing where no example is, which is a different
	# mistake from a misspelled fence and is named as that
	pub orphan: bool

	# an example whose fence lines are not all marked alike — some `##`, some `#`. The runner
	# reads the whole comment and runs it whole, while the reader's page shows only its `##`
	# lines, so such an example is checked as one thing and shown as a fragment of it.
	pub mixed: bool
}

# DocExample is one ` ```zerg ` fence and the ` ```output ` fence written directly under it, if
# there is one. With it the fence is an EXPRESSION example: each line is a value, printed. With
# none it is a STATEMENT example: each line is run as written, and it claims to print nothing
# — which is the only shape a function answering nothing can be shown working in.
pub struct DocExample {
	pub path: str

	# the line of the ` ```zerg ` fence itself, which is where a failure is reported
	pub line: int

	pub code: list[str]
	pub want: list[str]
	pub expr: bool
}

# DocFile is one source file of a module: where it was read from, what it says about itself,
# and whether it could be read at all.
pub struct DocFile {
	# the path it was read from, exactly as the caller resolved it. A stdlib file's is
	# absolute, because `stdlib_dir()` resolves against the executable; making it a name a
	# reader could type is a rendering decision and is made where the reader is known.
	pub path: str

	# the file header comment's READER text — its `##` lines, stripped of their markers; empty
	# when the file opens with a declaration, with a section banner, with nothing at all, or
	# with a header written for maintainers alone
	pub header: list[str]

	# the same header as written, both markers — what the maintainer's view prints
	pub written: list[str]

	# whether the file parsed. False is the exceptional case — `atomic` is the whole list
	# today — and it means the header above is all this file gave up: a renderer that said
	# nothing would turn a missing chapter into a silence.
	pub ok: bool
}

# DocDecl is one exposed declaration: what it is, what it is called, how it is called, and
# what was written above it.
pub struct DocDecl {
	# "fn" | "struct" | "enum" | "type" | "const" | "spec" — WHAT is declared, which is the
	# section a renderer files it under. It is a `str` and not an enum because the readers do
	# three things with it: group by it, print it, and compare it. An enum crossing the module
	# boundary would have to publish every variant to buy a spelling check on six words that
	# are already a string beside them.
	#
	# It is the word `sig` leads with wherever the language HAS a keyword for the form, but it
	# is not promised to be: a signature may carry a modifier in front of it (`unsafe fn`), and
	# a module-level binding is `const`, `mut`, or no keyword at all while all three are filed
	# under "const". A renderer that emphasises the name checks before it counts.
	pub kind: str

	# the bare declared name — `split`, `Logger`, `level`
	pub name: str

	# a method's receiver type, "" for everything else. The parser flattens `impl T { … }`
	# into ordinary functions carrying a receiver (ast.zg, FnDecl), and this follows the AST
	# rather than re-nesting them: a method is a top-level DocDecl with `recv` set, which the
	# renderer groups under its type and `doc_find` reaches as `Logger.level` in one scan.
	pub recv: str

	# the signature as plain text, `pub` omitted. Everything in a document is exposed by
	# definition, so the marker is noise on every line; dropping it also leaves all six forms
	# beginning with their keyword, which is what lets a renderer find the name to emphasise
	# by counting characters instead of parsing the string back.
	pub sig: str

	# the attached comment's READER text — its `##` lines, stripped; empty means UNDOCUMENTED,
	# and a renderer must say so rather than leave a gap the reader cannot tell from a
	# rendering bug. Every summary, the index, `--check` and a hover read this and nothing else.
	pub doc: list[str]

	# the attached comment as written, both markers — what the maintainer's view (`--all`)
	# prints. It is a second field rather than a flag on each line of `doc` because every
	# reader of `doc` would otherwise have to remember to filter, and the one that forgot
	# would publish the maintainer's notes.
	pub written: list[str]

	# the struct's public fields, the enum's variants, or the spec's required signatures —
	# each a line and its own comment. It is deliberately one level deep: none of the three
	# has anything below it, and a recursive container would invite a shape no source has.
	pub members: list[DocMember]

	# how many fields were NOT listed because they are private. Go's `contains unexported
	# fields` note, for the same reason: they are not interface, but a document that showed a
	# struct with two of its six fields would be describing a constructor call that does not
	# compile. The count is taken here, where every field is visible; the wording is the
	# renderer's.
	pub hidden: int

	# where it was declared. The `--check` that runs a document's examples has to report a
	# failure at a place, and the place is in hand exactly here — afterwards nobody can
	# recover it. This is ConstDecl's argument for carrying its own position, one layer up.
	pub path: str
	pub line: int
}

# DocMember is one field, variant, or required signature: a line, and whatever was written
# above it.
pub struct DocMember {
	# the bare declared name, and the line it was written on — what `doc_decl_at` finds one
	# BY, since a cursor arrives as a place and a member is not in `DocModule.decls`. The
	# renderer needs neither: it prints the whole list under the type that holds it.
	pub name: str

	pub sig: str

	# its reader text and its comment as written, exactly as `DocDecl` carries them
	pub doc: list[str]
	pub written: list[str]

	pub line: int
}

# --- extraction -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# doc_module extracts one module's document from its sources. `srcs` and `paths` are row for
# row — the pairing `load_sources_at` already keeps — because attaching a comment is per-FILE
# work and a file that will not parse has to be named by whatever says so.
#
# EVERY FILE IS PARSED UNDER `guard`. A module that cannot be parsed must still yield its
# header and an honest marker, never a silent absence; `atomic` is that module today. The
# diagnostic's own text cannot come back through a `guard`, so what is recorded is the fact —
# this file did not parse — and not a sentence about it.
pub fn doc_module(name: str, srcs: list[str], paths: list[str]) -> DocModule {
	# the two lists are ONE table, and a caller that hands over a short one would otherwise
	# leave an `IndexError` to come out of a `pub` function with nothing said about whose
	# mistake it was
	raise parser.p_impossible(f"doc_module was given {srcs.len()} sources and {paths.len()} paths — they are one table, a source and the file it was read from, and a document cannot name a file it was not told about") if srcs.len() != paths.len()

	mut files: list[DocFile] = []
	mut decls: list[DocDecl] = []
	mut examples: list[DocExample] = []
	mut strays: list[DocStray] = []

	mut i := 0
	for i < srcs.len() {
		path := paths[i]
		mut tv := doc_trivia(srcs[i])
		ok := doc_file_rows(srcs[i], path, false, tv, decls)

		# after the claim pass, never before it: a header is the run no declaration wanted
		head := doc_header(tv)
		files.append(DocFile(path, doc_reader(head), head.lines, ok))
		doc_examples(tv, path, examples, strays)
		i = i + 1
	}
	return DocModule(name, files, decls, examples, strays)
}

# doc_examples appends the examples one file's comments hold, and the lines that only look like
# the start of one. A fence never spans two runs: a blank line ends a run, and an example with a
# blank line inside it is two paragraphs of prose to a reader too.
#
# A FENCE IS EXACTLY ` ```zerg ` OR ` ```output ` — the whole comment once `# ` is taken off. That
# is one spelling to learn and one to check, and every other line that opens a fence naming
# zerg, zg or output — however it is spelled (`doc_fence_shaped`) — is reported rather than
# guessed at.
fn doc_examples(tv: DocTrivia, path: str, mut &out: list[DocExample], mut &strays: list[DocStray]) {
	for r in tv.runs {
		mut i := 0
		for i < r.lines.len() {
			if r.lines[i] != "```zerg" {
				if doc_fence_shaped(r.lines[i]) {
					strays.append(DocStray(path, r.first + i, r.lines[i], r.lines[i] == "```output", false))
				}
				i = i + 1
				continue
			}

			open := i
			at := r.first + i
			mut code: list[str] = []
			i = doc_fence_body(r.lines, i + 1, code)
			mut want: list[str] = []
			expr := i < r.lines.len() and r.lines[i] == "```output"
			if expr {
				i = doc_fence_body(r.lines, i + 1, want)
			}
			out.append(DocExample(path, at, code, want, expr))
			if not doc_alike(r.reader, open, i) {
				strays.append(DocStray(path, at, r.lines[open], false, true))
			}
		}
	}
}

# doc_alike reports whether the lines of a run from `lo` up to `hi` — one example, both its
# fences — carry the same marker. `hi` may run one past the end, where an example was never
# closed.
fn doc_alike(reader: list[bool], lo: int, hi: int) -> bool {
	mut k := lo + 1
	for k < hi and k < reader.len() {
		return false if reader[k] != reader[lo]
		k = k + 1
	}
	return true
}

# doc_fence_shaped reports whether a comment line opens a fence of any spelling — indented or
# not, three or more backticks or tildes, space or none before the word — whose word starts with
# `zerg`, `zg` or `output` in any case. That is every line a Markdown renderer might show as one
# of these fences, which is the population a reader would take for an example. Asked only of
# lines that are not an opening fence, so every line it answers true for is a stray.
fn doc_fence_shaped(line: str) -> bool {
	b := bytearray(line)
	mut i := 0
	for i < b.len() and (b[i] == b' ' or b[i] == b'\t') {
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false if i >= b.len() or (b[i] != b'`' and b[i] != b'~')

	mark := b[i]
	start := i
	for i < b.len() and b[i] == mark {
		i = i + 1
	}
	return false if i - start < 3

	for i < b.len() and (b[i] == b' ' or b[i] == b'\t') {
		i = i + 1
	}
	mut word: list[byte] = []
	for i < b.len() {
		word.append(ascii.fold_lower(b[i]))
		i = i + 1
	}
	rest := str(word)
	return parser.strings_has_prefix(rest, "zerg") or parser.strings_has_prefix(rest, "zg") or parser.strings_has_prefix(rest, "output")
}

# doc_fence_body collects the lines of a fence opened on the line before `i`, up to its closing
# ```, and answers the line after that close.
fn doc_fence_body(lines: list[str], i: int, mut &body: list[str]) -> int {
	mut k := i
	for k < lines.len() and lines[k] != "```" {
		body.append(lines[k])
		k = k + 1
	}
	return k + 1
}

# doc_find answers WHERE in `m.decls` the named declaration is. `name` is `split` or
# `Logger.level` — a method is reached by the same key the renderer groups it under, and the
# comparison is case sensitive because an identifier is.
#
# It answers EVERY declaration with that key, not the first. One key can name two declarations:
# a module may hold `pub struct Pair` and `pub fn Pair`, which `zerg lint` accepts and which
# the language keeps apart by position, and a directory module is several files whose names
# only have to be unique to the linker. Answering the first one in source order made the second
# unreachable AND unmentioned — `zerg doc mod.Pair` printed the struct and said nothing about
# the function — which is the silent omission this whole tool exists to prevent. What to do
# with two answers is the command's; what there are is this.
#
# It answers INDICES rather than `DocDecl` rows so that the caller can render a hit together
# with what belongs to it: a type's entry is the type plus the methods that follow it in the
# same list, which needs the position and not just the row.
pub fn doc_find(m: DocModule, name: str) -> list[int] {
	mut out: list[int] = []
	mut i := 0
	for i < m.decls.len() {
		d := m.decls[i]
		mut key := d.name
		if d.recv != "" {
			key = f"{d.recv}.{d.name}"
		}
		if key == name {
			out.append(i)
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return out
}

# DocAt is one declaration as a document holds it — its signature and the comment above it —
# found by WHERE ITS NAME IS WRITTEN rather than by what it is called (#192: hover is the
# document of the name at the cursor).
#
# `ok` is whether there is an entry at all. A document covers declarations, and a cursor lands
# on anything a program writes: a local binding, a parameter, a type parameter, the namespace
# an import bound. None of those is in any document, and marking them undocumented would be a
# claim about a comment nobody could have written — so they answer `ok` false, and what to say
# about a name with no entry is the caller's.
#
# A SOURCE THAT COULD NOT BE READ answers `ok` false as well, and deliberately: a file handed
# over empty, or one that will not parse, has no entry to give, and saying so as an absence is
# the same sentence as "no document covers this". What a reader may NOT conclude from `ok`
# false is that nothing was written — only that this extraction has nothing to show.
pub struct DocAt {
	pub ok: bool

	# the signature as the document prints it — a declaration's, or the line a field, a variant
	# or a spec's requirement is written as
	pub sig: str

	# the attached comment, stripped; empty means UNDOCUMENTED, exactly as `DocDecl.doc` does
	pub doc: list[str]

	# whether the page MARKS the absence here — `doc_marks_absence`, which is the ONE author of
	# that rule and which `zerg doc`'s renderer asks as well. A document marks a declaration
	# nobody wrote about `(undocumented)` and prints a member with no comment BARE; a reader
	# that decided it for itself would be a second author, and the two would come to disagree
	# about half of what a page shows without anyone editing either of them.
	pub mark: bool
}

# doc_decl_at is the document of the declaration whose name is written at `line` in `src`, or
# `ok` false. It is the SAME EXTRACTION `zerg doc` prints from, asked for one place instead of
# a whole module: the same comment attachment (`doc_attach`), the same signature printer, the
# same mark where nothing was written. A second reader of comments — in a server, or anywhere
# else — would be a document the terminal does not have and a document the editor does not
# share.
#
# It takes a NAME as well as a line because one line can declare more than one thing and a
# `DocDecl` carries no column: the caller has the name (the index's declaration row does), and
# a line and a name together are a key nothing in one file repeats.
#
# MEMBERS ARE SEARCHED TOO — a field, a variant, a spec's requirement — because each is
# documented where it is written and a cursor reaches it exactly the way it reaches the type
# above it.
#
# THE FILE IS PARSED HERE, from source handed in. Nothing is cached between calls: a hover is
# a question a person asked, and one parse of one file is what it costs.
pub fn doc_decl_at(src: str, path: str, line: int, name: str) -> DocAt {
	mut none: list[str] = []
	mut tv := doc_trivia(src)
	mut ds: list[DocDecl] = []
	doc_file_rows(src, path, true, tv, ds)

	for d in ds {
		return DocAt(true, d.sig, d.doc, doc_marks_absence(false, d.doc)) if d.line == line and d.name == name

		for m in d.members {
			return DocAt(true, m.sig, m.doc, doc_marks_absence(true, m.doc)) if m.line == line and m.name == name
		}
	}
	return DocAt(false, "", none, false)
}

# doc_covers is whether a document can hold an entry for that kind of declaration (`ast.NK_*`).
# The walk above emits a const, a struct, an enum, a type, a spec, a function and the members
# of the three that have any — so what it answers NO for is the four a module's surface never
# holds: a binding, a parameter, a type parameter, and the namespace an import bound. Written
# as the four rather than as the nine, because a kind added to the language is a kind a
# document may well hold, and the honest default is to go and look.
#
# It is here because it is a fact about THIS extraction, and its caller is whoever would go and
# find a source to run the extraction over: `zerg lsp`'s hover asks first, and does not load a
# program to be told there was never going to be an entry.
pub fn doc_covers(kind: int) -> bool {
	return false if kind == ast.NK_PARAM or kind == ast.NK_LOCAL or kind == ast.NK_TPARAM or kind == ast.NK_IMPORT

	return true
}

# doc_marks_absence is whether a page prints the mark where no comment was written: beside a
# DECLARATION it does, and beside a MEMBER — a field, a variant, a spec's requirement — it does
# not, which is what a reader meets on a page today.
#
# It is one function because it had been two AGREEING PLACES: the renderer marked a declaration
# and printed a member bare, each in its own branch, and nothing said the two were one rule. A
# hover (#192) is the third reader of it, and a third place that happened to agree is how the
# editor and the terminal come to say different things about the same line without anyone
# editing either of them.
pub fn doc_marks_absence(member: bool, body: list[str]) -> bool {
	return false if member

	return body.len() == 0
}

# doc_undocumented is the mark that stands where a comment was not written. It is a WORD and
# not a rendering, which is why it is here: a document prints it in the middle of a page, an
# editor shows it in a hover, and the two saying different things would be two answers to
# "is this documented" — the question `zerg doc --check` exists to ask.
pub fn doc_undocumented() -> str {
	return "(undocumented)"
}

# doc_first_sentence is the one-line summary that a brief listing and a module index both
# show: the first paragraph flattened onto one line, cut at the first `.` that ends a word.
#
# It lives beside the extraction rather than in either renderer because both of them ask it,
# and issue #20's HTML will be the third — two copies of "what is a sentence" would drift into
# two different summaries of the same comment. A fence ends the paragraph: an example is not
# a description, and the first line of one reads as a fragment.
pub fn doc_first_sentence(doc: list[str]) -> str {
	mut para := ""
	for line in doc {
		break if line == ""
		break if doc_is_fence(line)

		if para != "" {
			para = para + " "
		}
		para = para + line
	}

	b := bytearray(para)
	mut i := 0
	for i < b.len() {
		if b[i] == b'.' {
			return parser.fstr_slice(b, 0, i + 1) if i + 1 == b.len() or b[i + 1] == b' '
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return para
}

fn doc_is_fence(line: str) -> bool {
	b := bytearray(line)
	return false if b.len() < 3
	return b[0] == b'`' and b[1] == b'`' and b[2] == b'`'
}

# doc_is_banner reports whether a comment line is a section banner — `--- levels ---------`,
# the form all two hundred of them in this tree are written in. It is asked of a run's FIRST
# line only, and the file header explains why the whole run goes with it.
#
# The shape is required at both ends: `--- ` opens it, and it closes on the dashes that pad it
# out to the source's right margin. A paragraph that happens to begin with an em-rule does not
# end in one, and a line of prose does not begin with three hyphens and a space.
fn doc_is_banner(line: str) -> bool {
	b := bytearray(line)

	return false if b.len() < 8
	return false if b[0] != b'-' or b[1] != b'-' or b[2] != b'-' or b[3] != b' '

	return b[b.len() - 1] == b'-' and b[b.len() - 2] == b'-' and b[b.len() - 3] == b'-'
}

# --- the declarations of one file -----------------------------------------------------------

# doc_file_decls walks one parsed file, claims a comment for EVERY declaration in it, and
# appends the exposed ones to `out` in source order.
#
# The private ones are walked for their claim alone, and `all` is what KEEPS them. A document
# is what a module exposes, so `doc_module` asks for the exposed ones and nothing else; a
# cursor is on whatever the author is looking at, so `doc_decl_at` asks for every declaration
# and picks the one at a place. One walk, one attachment rule, one signature printer, and the
# difference between the two questions is which rows come back.
#
# Walking the private ones is also what keeps `doc_header` honest — a run a private function
# owns is not the file's header — and it costs one pass either way. An `impl` head claims too:
# it declares nothing this document lists, but a comment above `impl Display for Meters` is
# about that impl, and if the impl were the first thing in a file the comment would otherwise
# be read as the module's header.
#
# The file's own lists are per KIND, so nothing in them is where the author left it; they are
# collected and then put back into source order by `doc_sorted`.
fn doc_file_decls(f: ast.File, mut &tv: DocTrivia, mut &out: list[DocDecl], all: bool) {
	mut ds: list[DocDecl] = []

	for c in f.consts {
		run := doc_attach(tv, c.line)
		mut none: list[DocMember] = []
		if c.is_pub or all {
			ds.append(DocDecl("const", c.name, "", doc_const_sig(c), doc_reader(run), run.lines, none, 0, c.path, c.line))
		}
	}

	for s in f.structs {
		run := doc_attach(tv, s.line)
		mut ms: list[DocMember] = []
		mut hidden := 0
		for fd in s.fields {
			fdoc := doc_attach(tv, fd.line)
			if fd.is_pub or all {
				ms.append(DocMember(fd.name, doc_field_sig(fd), doc_reader(fdoc), fdoc.lines, fd.line))
			} else {
				# `hidden` is how many were LEFT OUT, so it counts the ones this walk did not
				# list — under `all` that is none of them, and a row saying otherwise would
				# describe a constructor call the reader can see every field of.
				hidden = hidden + 1
			}
		}
		if s.is_pub or all {
			ds.append(DocDecl("struct", s.name, "", f"struct {s.name}{doc_tparams(s.tparams)}", doc_reader(run), run.lines, ms, hidden, s.path, s.line))
		}
	}

	for e in f.enums {
		run := doc_attach(tv, e.line)
		mut ms: list[DocMember] = []
		for vd in e.variants {
			vdoc := doc_attach(tv, vd.line)
			ms.append(DocMember(vd.name, doc_variant_sig(vd), doc_reader(vdoc), vdoc.lines, vd.line))
		}
		if e.is_pub or all {
			ds.append(DocDecl("enum", e.name, "", "enum " + e.name, doc_reader(run), run.lines, ms, 0, e.path, e.line))
		}
	}

	for t in f.types {
		run := doc_attach(tv, t.line)
		mut none: list[DocMember] = []
		if t.is_pub or all {
			ds.append(DocDecl("type", t.name, "", f"type {t.name} = {ast.ty_name(t.under)}", doc_reader(run), run.lines, none, 0, t.path, t.line))
		}
	}

	for sp in f.specs {
		run := doc_attach(tv, sp.line)
		mut ms: list[DocMember] = []
		for req in sp.reqs {
			rdoc := doc_attach(tv, req.line)
			ms.append(DocMember(req.name, doc_fn_sig(req), doc_reader(rdoc), rdoc.lines, req.line))
		}
		if sp.is_pub or all {
			ds.append(DocDecl("spec", sp.name, "", doc_spec_sig(sp), doc_reader(run), run.lines, ms, 0, sp.path, sp.line))
		}
	}

	for ip in f.impls {
		doc_claim(tv, ip.line)
	}

	for fd in f.fns {
		run := doc_attach(tv, fd.line)
		mut none: list[DocMember] = []
		if fd.is_pub or all {
			ds.append(DocDecl("fn", fd.name, fd.recv, doc_fn_sig(fd), doc_reader(run), run.lines, none, 0, fd.path, fd.line))
		}
	}

	for d in doc_sorted(ds) {
		out.append(d)
	}
}

# doc_file_rows is one file turned into rows: parse it, and claim a comment for everything in
# it. It answers whether it PARSED, and leaves the trivia with the caller — a module header is
# the run nothing claimed, which only a whole document asks about.
#
# EVERY FILE IS PARSED UNDER `guard`, and that is why this is one function rather than two
# call sites: a module that cannot be parsed must still yield its header and an honest marker,
# and a second reader with its own raise policy would answer differently about the same file.
fn doc_file_rows(src: str, path: str, all: bool, mut &tv: DocTrivia, mut &out: list[DocDecl]) -> bool {
	parsed := guard {
		parser.parse_at(src, path)
	}
	if f := parsed {
		doc_file_decls(f, tv, out, all)
		return true
	}
	return false
}

# doc_sorted puts one file's declarations back into source order.
#
# Insertion sort by adjacent swaps, for the reason `sym_sorted` (ast.zg) gives about the same
# job on the same data: a file's top level is tens of declarations, and what matters is that
# the sort is STABLE, so two declarations reported at one line keep the order they were
# collected in instead of trading places between runs.
fn doc_sorted(ds: list[DocDecl]) -> list[DocDecl] {
	mut out: list[DocDecl] = []
	for d in ds {
		out.append(d)
		mut j := out.len() - 1
		for j > 0 {
			break if out[j].line >= out[j - 1].line
			tmp := out[j - 1]
			out[j - 1] = out[j]
			out[j] = tmp
			j = j - 1
		}
	}
	return out
}

# --- signatures -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# doc_fn_sig spells a function the way its declaration is written, without the `pub`.
#
# A METHOD DROPS ITS FIRST PARAMETER: the parser prepends `this: T` when it flattens an
# `impl`, so printing the list as the AST holds it would show a receiver nobody writes at the
# call site. The receiver survives as `DocDecl.recv`, which is where the renderer reads it,
# and its MUTABILITY survives as the word `mut` — see `doc_fn_word`, which reads that fact out
# of the very parameter this drops.
#
# A FUNCTION DECLARED IN A MODULE-LEVEL `unsafe { … }` GROUP IS AN `unsafe fn`, and says so.
# GRAMMAR group 12 gives the group that meaning — "unsafe throughout its body, callable only
# from unsafe" — and group 5 gives the single-function form the same spelling, so this is the
# language's own word for it and not a marker invented here. Leaving it off would document a
# function as callable from anywhere when it is callable from one place.
# doc_tparams is a declaration's type parameters, spelled. A generic `fn` printed its own and a
# generic STRUCT did not, so `struct Box[T]` was documented as `struct Box` with a field `v: T`
# — a parameter with no introduction, in the one document a reader has for the type.
fn doc_tparams(tps: list[str]) -> str {
	return "" if tps.len() == 0

	return ast.dump_tparams(tps)
}

fn doc_fn_sig(fd: ast.FnDecl) -> str {
	mut out := doc_fn_word(fd) + fd.name
	if fd.tparams.len() > 0 {
		out = out + "["
		mut i := 0
		for i < fd.tparams.len() {
			if i > 0 {
				out = out + ", "
			}
			out = out + fd.tparams[i]
			if fd.tbounds[i] != "" {
				out = out + ": " + doc_bound(fd.tbounds[i])
			}
			i = i + 1
		}
		out = out + "]"
	}

	out = out + "("
	mut k := 0
	if fd.recv != "" {
		k = 1
	}
	mut first := true
	for k < fd.params.len() {
		p := fd.params[k]
		if not first {
			out = out + ", "
		}
		first = false
		if p.is_ref {
			out = out + "mut &"
		}
		out = out + p.name + ": " + ast.ty_name(p.ty)
		if p.has_def {
			out = out + " = " + doc_lit(p.def)
		}
		k = k + 1
	}
	out = out + ")"

	return f"{out} -> {ast.ty_name(fd.ret)}" if fd.has_ret
	return out
}

# doc_fn_word spells the words that stand between `pub` and the name, in the order
# GRAMMAR#fn-decl derives them: `'pub'? 'unsafe'? 'mut'? 'fn'`.
#
# `mut fn` IS READ OUT OF THE PARAMETER THE SIGNATURE DROPS. The parser encodes it as the
# prepended `this` being a MUTABLE REFERENCE (`parse_fn_head`), so a method's `mut` lives
# nowhere but the parameter `doc_fn_sig` skips. Leaving it off is the lie a dropped `mut`
# binding would be, and a sharper one: GRAMMAR#fn-decl says the CALL SITE must hold the
# receiver in a `mut` binding, so a signature printed without the word tells a reader they may
# call the method on an immutable binding — and the compiler then refuses the program the
# document described.
fn doc_fn_word(fd: ast.FnDecl) -> str {
	mut out := ""
	if fd.in_unsafe {
		out = out + "unsafe "
	}
	if doc_is_mut_fn(fd) {
		out = out + "mut "
	}
	return out + "fn "
}

# doc_is_mut_fn answers whether a declaration is a `mut fn`. Only a method can be one — a free
# function has no receiver to mutate — so `recv` is asked first, and the answer is then the
# receiver's own `mut &`.
fn doc_is_mut_fn(fd: ast.FnDecl) -> bool {
	return false if fd.recv == ""
	return fd.params[0].is_ref
}

# doc_bound reads a type parameter's bound back into the source's spelling. A bound is a
# CONJUNCTION carried joined by `+` in one string (ast.zg, FnDecl.tbounds), and `c_split_bounds`
# is the one function that takes it apart — the emitter's, because that is where a bound is
# checked, and a second splitter would be a second opinion about where a spec name ends.
fn doc_bound(bound: str) -> str {
	mut out := ""
	for sp in emit.c_split_bounds(bound) {
		if out != "" {
			out = out + " + "
		}
		out = out + sp
	}
	return out
}

# doc_const_sig spells a module-level binding with the keyword that was WRITTEN, and with none
# when none was.
#
# It used to print `const` for all three forms, on the argument that a module-level binding is
# the module's immutable state (docs/runtime/package.md). A `mut` binding inside a module-level
# `unsafe { … }` group is precisely what that argument is the exception to (GRAMMAR group 12),
# so the one mutable global the language has was documented as the one thing it is not.
#
# `in_unsafe` is deliberately NOT spelled here, and it is the only fact about the declaration
# this signature leaves out. GRAMMAR group 12 says in as many words that there is no `unsafe
# mut` prefix, so writing one would put into a document a form the language will not read; and
# what the group grants a binding is exactly the `mut`, which is already the first word. A
# function is the other way round — `unsafe fn` IS a spelling (group 5) — which is why
# `doc_fn_sig` prints it and this does not.
fn doc_const_sig(c: ast.ConstDecl) -> str {
	head := doc_bind_word(c) + c.name
	return f"{head}: {ast.ty_name(c.ty)} = {doc_lit(c.value)}" if c.has_ty
	return f"{head} := {doc_lit(c.value)}"
}

fn doc_bind_word(c: ast.ConstDecl) -> str {
	return "const " if c.is_const
	return "mut " if c.is_mut

	return ""
}

fn doc_field_sig(fd: ast.FieldDef) -> str {
	out := f"{fd.name}: {ast.ty_name(fd.ty)}"
	return f"{out} = {doc_lit(fd.def)}" if fd.has_def
	return out
}

fn doc_variant_sig(vd: ast.VariantDef) -> str {
	mut out := vd.name
	if vd.fields.len() > 0 {
		out = out + "(" + ast.ty_names(vd.fields) + ")"
	}
	return f"{out} = {doc_lit(vd.discr)}" if vd.has_discr
	return out
}

fn doc_spec_sig(sp: ast.SpecDecl) -> str {
	mut out := "spec " + sp.name
	if sp.params.len() > 0 {
		out = out + "[" + parser.join_commas(sp.params) + "]"
	}
	mut i := 0
	for i < sp.supers.len() {
		if i == 0 {
			out = out + ": "
		} else {
			out = out + " + "
		}
		out = out + ast.spec_key(sp.supers[i], sp.super_args[i])
		i = i + 1
	}
	return out
}

# doc_lit prints a default value when it IS a literal and `…` when it is not.
#
# It reads the surface text the literal node already kept (the invariant ast.zg opens with) and
# it does not recurse: a call, a name, a negation are all `…`. That is not a gap to be filled
# later — a signature in a document says how to CALL something, and a default built out of an
# expression is a fact about the source, which the `…` sends the reader to.
fn doc_lit(e: ast.Expr) -> str {
	return match e {
		Expr.EInt(lex, _, _) => lex
		Expr.EFloat(lex) => lex
		Expr.EStr(v) => f"\"{v}\""
		Expr.EBool(v) => doc_bool(v)
		Expr.ENil => "nil"
		_ => "…"
	}
}

fn doc_bool(v: bool) -> str {
	return "true" if v
	return "false"
}

# --- comments, on the token stream --------------------------------------------------------

# DocRun is one block of whole-line comments on consecutive lines — a candidate for being some
# declaration's documentation, or the file's header.
struct DocRun {
	pub first: int
	pub last: int
	pub lines: list[str]

	# row for row with `lines`: whether that line was written `##`, the reader's marker
	pub reader: list[bool]
}

# DocTrivia is the whole first pass: the runs a file holds, and two facts per LINE that decide
# which declaration each run belongs to. `taken` runs beside `runs` and records the claim, so
# a run answers exactly one declaration and `doc_header` can ask what is left.
#
# `code`, `deco` and `imp` are indexed BY LINE NUMBER, one slot per line of the file plus a
# spare, so the arithmetic below can read the line above or below without a bound test at every
# step.
struct DocTrivia {
	pub runs: list[DocRun]
	pub taken: list[bool]
	pub code: list[bool]
	pub deco: list[bool]

	# whether the line holds an `import`. It is asked lexically, off Kind.Import, rather than
	# from `File.imports`, so that a file whose parse RAISED can still be asked — which is the
	# only condition under which a module has a header and no declarations at all.
	pub imp: list[bool]
}

# doc_trivia is the first pass: one walk of the lexed-with-comments stream.
#
# A `#[…]` decorator is Kind.Hash and not a comment — the lexer separated them long before
# this — and its lines are marked as BOTH code and decorator: code, because something is
# written there, and decorator, because the claim in pass two steps over exactly those lines.
# The bracket depth is what carries a decorator that spans lines.
#
# The ASI semicolons and the Eof are neither: they are punctuation the lexer inserted, and a
# line that holds nothing else is a blank line to a reader.
fn doc_trivia(src: str) -> DocTrivia {
	toks := lexer.tokenize_trivia(src)

	mut last_line := 1
	for t in toks {
		if t.line > last_line {
			last_line = t.line
		}
	}

	mut code: list[bool] = []
	mut deco: list[bool] = []
	mut imp: list[bool] = []
	mut n := 0
	for n <= last_line + 1 {
		code.append(false)
		deco.append(false)
		imp.append(false)
		n = n + 1
	}

	mut runs: list[DocRun] = []
	mut taken: list[bool] = []
	mut prev := 0
	mut depth := 0

	for t in toks {
		if depth > 0 {
			code[t.line] = true
			deco[t.line] = true
			prev = t.line
			if t.kind == token.Kind.LBrack {
				depth = depth + 1
			} else if t.kind == token.Kind.RBrack {
				depth = depth - 1
			}
			continue
		}

		if t.kind == token.Kind.Comment {
			# a note at the end of a line of code, which documents nothing below it
			continue if prev == t.line

			doc_add_line(runs, taken, t.line, doc_strip(t.lexeme), doc_is_reader(t.lexeme))
			continue
		}

		continue if t.kind == token.Kind.Semi or t.kind == token.Kind.Eof

		code[t.line] = true
		prev = t.line
		if t.kind == token.Kind.Hash {
			deco[t.line] = true
			depth = 1
		} else if t.kind == token.Kind.Import {
			imp[t.line] = true
		}
	}
	return DocTrivia(runs, taken, code, deco, imp)
}

# doc_add_line puts one comment line into the run it continues, or opens a new run. A run is
# extended only when the line ABOVE was the previous comment line, which is what makes a blank
# line a separator without anything here looking at whitespace.
fn doc_add_line(mut &runs: list[DocRun], mut &taken: list[bool], line: int, text: str, reader: bool) {
	if runs.len() > 0 {
		k := runs.len() - 1
		if runs[k].last == line - 1 {
			mut r := runs[k]
			r.last = line
			r.lines.append(text)
			r.reader.append(reader)
			runs[k] = r
			return
		}
	}

	mut ls: list[str] = []
	ls.append(text)
	mut rs: list[bool] = []
	rs.append(reader)
	runs.append(DocRun(line, line, ls, rs))
	taken.append(false)
}

# doc_is_reader reports whether a comment is the reader's — written `##`, a second `#` with no
# space before it. `# ## Heading` is the maintainer's: the space after the first `#` ends the
# marker, the same line `doc_strip` draws.
fn doc_is_reader(lexeme: str) -> bool {
	b := bytearray(lexeme)
	return b.len() >= 2 and b[0] == b'#' and b[1] == b'#'
}

# doc_reader is the reader's text of one run: its `##` lines, in order, and nothing else.
#
# WHERE A NOTE WAS TAKEN OUT, A PARAGRAPH ENDS. `## a`, `# why`, `## b` is two remarks the
# author kept apart with a note between them, and running `a` and `b` together would fill them
# into one sentence nobody wrote. A blank line either side of the note is the same break, so
# it is never doubled, and none is left at either end of the text. Inside a fence nothing is
# joined or dropped: its lines are an example's, blank ones included.
fn doc_reader(r: DocRun) -> list[str] {
	mut out: list[str] = []
	mut cut := false
	mut fence := false
	mut i := 0
	for i < r.lines.len() {
		text := r.lines[i]
		i = i + 1
		if not r.reader[i - 1] {
			cut = true
			continue
		}
		if fence {
			out.append(text)
			fence = not doc_is_fence(text)
			continue
		}
		if text == "" or cut {
			if out.len() > 0 and out[out.len() - 1] != "" {
				out.append("")
			}
			cut = false
			continue if text == ""
		}
		out.append(text)
		fence = doc_is_fence(text)
	}
	mut n := out.len()
	for n > 0 and out[n - 1] == "" {
		n = n - 1
	}
	return out if n == out.len()

	mut kept: list[str] = []
	mut k := 0
	for k < n {
		kept.append(out[k])
		k = k + 1
	}
	return kept
}

# doc_strip takes the `#` off a comment, and one space after it, and any trailing whitespace.
# What is left is the text as written — the fence lines, the indentation of an example, and
# the blank line between two paragraphs all survive, because they are what the author put
# there and the renderer needs them to tell prose from a code block.
#
# A SECOND `#` IS THE DOC-COMMENT MARKER and comes off with the first (#93). `GRAMMAR` gives
# `##` a production of its own — DOC-COMMENT, attached to the declaration that follows — and
# this compiler lexes it as an ordinary comment whose text happens to begin with a `#`; which
# reader a line is for is `doc_is_reader`'s question, asked of the same lexeme. Leaving the
# marker ON rendered `## doubles its argument.` as `# doubles its argument.` — the marker
# leaking into the prose it marks.
#
# ONLY A `#` WITH NO SPACE BEFORE IT is the marker. `# ## Heading` is a markdown heading a
# author wrote inside ordinary prose: the space after the first `#` ends the marker, and the
# `##` that follows is text. That is why the space is tested after the second hash and not
# before it.
fn doc_strip(lexeme: str) -> str {
	b := bytearray(lexeme)
	mut lo := 0
	if lo < b.len() and b[lo] == b'#' {
		lo = lo + 1
	}
	if lo < b.len() and b[lo] == b'#' {
		lo = lo + 1
	}
	if lo < b.len() and b[lo] == b' ' {
		lo = lo + 1
	}
	mut hi := b.len()
	for hi > lo and (b[hi - 1] == b' ' or b[hi - 1] == b'\t') {
		hi = hi - 1
	}
	return parser.fstr_slice(b, lo, hi)
}

# doc_attach is pass two for one declaration: the run that ends on the line above it, stepping
# over the decorator lines in between, or nothing when the line above holds code or a blank.
#
# One run can answer only one declaration, and it cannot answer two by accident: a run's last
# line is unique, so at most one declaration is on the line below it. `taken` records the claim
# for `doc_header`, which is the only thing that asks about the runs nobody wanted.
fn doc_attach(mut &tv: DocTrivia, line: int) -> DocRun {
	at := doc_claim(tv, line)
	return doc_no_run() if at < 0

	# claimed and then thrown away: a section banner is taken OUT of circulation, so that
	# `doc_header` cannot pick it up either, and answers nothing
	return doc_no_run() if doc_is_banner(tv.runs[at].lines[0])

	return tv.runs[at]
}

# doc_no_run is the comment that was not written: no line, in either view.
fn doc_no_run() -> DocRun {
	mut ls: list[str] = []
	mut rs: list[bool] = []
	return DocRun(0, 0, ls, rs)
}

# doc_claim is doc_attach without the text — the index of the run claimed, or -1. It is what an
# `impl` head calls: an impl takes its comment out of circulation without putting it in the
# document.
fn doc_claim(mut &tv: DocTrivia, line: int) -> int {
	mut a := line
	for a > 1 and tv.deco[a - 1] {
		a = a - 1
	}
	return -1 if a < 2

	mut i := 0
	for i < tv.runs.len() {
		if tv.runs[i].last == a - 1 and not tv.taken[i] {
			tv.taken[i] = true
			return i
		}
		i = i + 1
	}
	return -1
}

# doc_header is the module's own comment: the FIRST run in the file, with no code above it,
# that no declaration claimed, that is not a section banner, and with nothing but a blank line
# or an `import` under it.
#
# Each of the four conditions is doing work. FIRST, because a comment further down a file is
# about what is under it. NO CODE ABOVE, because a comment written below the first declaration
# is a remark inside the file and not a description of it. UNCLAIMED, because a file may open
# with a comment that documents its first declaration — `has_prefix`, example and all, is the
# first thing in `strings.zg` after the header — and that comment belongs to the function,
# leaving the module with no header, which is the honest answer rather than a stolen one.
#
# THE LINE BELOW is the fourth, and it is there because pass two cannot see everything that can
# be written. An INHERENT `impl T { … }` head is not in the parsed file at all: the parser
# flattens the block into methods and records an ImplDecl only for `impl Spec for T`, so a
# comment above `impl Thing {` is claimed by nobody, and the first three conditions alone would
# read it as the module's header — a remark about one type presented as the document's opening
# paragraph. Nothing may be under a header but a blank line or an import.
#
# The import half of that is not a concession but the case that fixed the whole rule:
# `strings.zg`'s header runs straight into `import "ascii"` with no blank line between them, so
# a rule phrased as "separated by a blank line" gives the most-read module in the standard
# library no header at all. An import declares no member of this module's surface and claims no
# comment; a reader looking above it is reading about the file.
fn doc_header(tv: DocTrivia) -> DocRun {
	return doc_no_run() if tv.runs.len() == 0
	return doc_no_run() if tv.taken[0]

	r := tv.runs[0]
	return doc_no_run() if doc_is_banner(r.lines[0])

	mut i := 1
	for i < r.first {
		return doc_no_run() if tv.code[i]
		i = i + 1
	}

	below := r.last + 1
	return doc_no_run() if tv.code[below] and not tv.imp[below]
	return r
}
